## Introduction
De novo protein design provides one of the most stringent tests of the extent of our understanding of protein folding and structure. Several groups have developed and experimentally tested systematic quantitative methods for protein design directed toward the idea of a fully automated process.1–3 These techniques, which have been used to screen possible sequences for compatibility with the desired protein fold, have been focused mostly on the re-design of protein cores. In very few cases, the successful automatic design of an original sequence for an entire protein has been achieved.2,3
An especially important challenge in protein design is the generation of well-folded all-\(\upbeta\)-sheet proteins. Until now, the available information on the determinants of \(\upbeta\)-sheet stability has been obtained from systematic mutagenesis experiments, through the study of simple de novo designed \(\upbeta\)-hairpin peptides and, more recently, from several peptides that adopt monomeric three-stranded antiparallel \(\upbeta\)-sheets in aqueous solution.4–7 The simplicity of these peptides makes them attractive model molecules for the study of the factors underlying \(\upbeta\)-sheet formation. In fact, several molecular dynamic studies have been performed on them and other molecules that adopt \(\upbeta\)-hairpin conformation in aqueous solution.8–11
We now report on the automatic re-design of the sequence of one of these \(\upbeta\)-sheet peptides, Betanova.5 PERLA, a protein design algorithm,12,13 was used to define automatically both single and multiple-residue mutations of the peptide. With the expectation of evaluating population differences between the designed peptides, and having the final goal of comparing the scale of predicted and observed stabilities, we selected a subset of mutants and analysed their conformational behaviour by \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectroscopy and far-UV CD.
## Results
## Computer-aided design cycle of new \(\upbeta\)-sheet peptides
We have re-designed the sequence of Betanova with an automatic design algorithm, PERLA. A detailed description of the algorithm is available elsewhere† (see Methods).12,13 Here, we just summarise some aspects of the program. This algorithm enables the identification and sorting of amino acid sequences capable of folding into a desired three-dimensional structure. The ability of an amino acid sequence to fit a protein structure is evaluated through a scoring function that uses an all-atom molecular mechanics force field and a combination of statistical terms, including entropy and solvation. The program performs strict inverse folding: a fixed backbone structure is decorated with amino acid side-chains from a rotamer library. Relaxation of the strains in the protein structure is achieved via the generation of subrotamers. Most terms of the scoring function are balanced with respect to a reference state to simulate the denatured protein. Dead-end elimination can be used to reduce the sequence space.14 Then, side-chain conformers are all weighted using the mean field theory.15 PERLA finally produces candidate sequences with modelled structures and energy descriptions.
For the work presented here, PERLA calculations were carried out using as template the three-dimensional model employed to design Betanova.5 First, only single-residue mutations were considered. We modelled all 20 amino acids at positions 5, 12, 15 and 17 (Figure 1), where side-chain interactions take place with Trp3 and Tyr10. The underlying reason behind this decision was to use nuclear Overhauser effects (NOEs) between these aromatic amino acids and the modelled residues as probes into the evaluation of the structural effects of the mutations. Since wild-type Lys15 was ranked among the best possible amino acids by PERLA, position 15 was eventually ignored in our study (Table 1). At positions 5, 12 and 17, the wildtype residues, respectively Val, Asn and Thr, were ranked at about the middle of the 20 amino acid scale. Val5 and Thr17 were mutated, separately, either into Asp, Arg, Tyr or Leu. According to the sequence-to-structure fitness score evaluated by PERLA, this set of eight peptides should comprise both stabilising and destabilising mutations (Table 1). At position 12, Asn was mutated into Leu and Phe. Regarding our calculations, these new sequences should have higher propensity to fold than Betanova (Table 1).
Second, multiple-residue mutations were designed with PERLA, modelling all 20 amino acids at positions 5 and 17 simultaneously, and fix
ing Leu12. The most promising sequences (Leu5, Phe5 or Tyr5 together with Met17) in terms of energy (Table 2), were selected and the corresponding peptides synthesised. The relative contribution of the different terms of the energy function used by PERLA to the total predicted energy is shown in Table 2. Typically, van der Waals forces are the major contributor to the stability.
