## CD and NMR analysis of the \(\upbeta\) -sheet population
Far-UV CD spectroscopy provides a rapid and sensitive analysis of secondary structure propensity in peptides. Nevertheless, the overlapping of the backbone amide and side-chain aromatic chromophores in the far-UV region makes such an analysis complicated for peptides that contain aromatic residues.21 Therefore, only peptides containing the same aromatic residues were compared in terms of their \(\upbeta\) -sheet content using CD data.
In aqueous solution, the designed peptides with the same aromatic content (except R5 and D5) displayed a CD spectrum with two minima: one around \(217~\mathrm{nm}\) ( \(\upbeta\) -sheet contribution to the CD spectrum) and another one between 198 and \(206~\mathrm{nm}\) (random coil contribution) (not shown). This indicated the presence of both \(\upbeta\) -structure and unfolded conformations in solution. Upon addition
of methanol,22–25 the spectra of most mutants showed an increase of the percentage of folded \(\upbeta\) -sheet conformations in solution. For most peptides, the titration reached a plateau in \(40\,\%\) \((\mathrm{v/v})\) aqueous methanol and showed an isodichroic point at \(209\ \mathrm{nm}\) (see Figure 4 for an example).
To compare the \(\upbeta\) -sheet content of the peptides, we calculated the ratio between the ellipticity at \(217~\mathrm{nm}\) and \(204~\mathrm{nm}\) (see ratio \(R_{217/204}\) in Table 4).26 This ratio is concentration-independent and is related to the \(\upbeta\) -sheet content in solution. Eventually, comparison of the CD data of the peptides with the same content of aromatic residues indicated that peptide LLM is the most structured, followed by L12, whilst D5 and R17 are mostly unstructured. The original Betanova is approximately at the middle of the \(R_{217/204}\) ranking list.
The difference between the observed \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) \((\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{H}\alpha})\) , HN \((\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{HN}})\) and \(^{13}\mathrm{C}\) \((\Delta\delta_{13\mathrm{C}\alpha})\) NMR chemical shifts and the chemical shifts of an appropriate random coil reference peptide is characteristic of secondary structure formation and can provide a method of estimating the population of folded structures. We used both \(\mathrm{RMS}\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{H}\alpha}\) values taken over all \(\upbeta\) -strand residues19 and \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{H}\alpha}\) of three residues of the central \(\upbeta\) -strand (Lys9, Tyr10 and Thr11) as a measure of stability. These three residues were selected because they are present in all peptides and the random coil reference peptides corresponding to
the central strand were available for all mutants, apart from F12. \(\beta\)-Sheet populations and \(K_{\mathrm{eq}}\) for \(\beta\)-sheet formation (see Methods)\(^{27}\) were estimated assuming a two-state transition (Table 5). As shown in Table 5, similar results were obtained using different probes to estimate the \(\beta\)-sheet population. The most stabilising single-point mutations are Val5Tyr and Thr17Tyr. These mutants present a \(\beta\)-sheet population of \(16{-}20\,\%\) and \(13{-}17\,\%\), respectively, in pure aqueous solutions. The other single-point mutants are either highly unstable or present similar stability to the original Betanova. Mutations at the second \(\beta\)-strand, Asn12Phe and Asn12Leu, led also to peptides with higher \(\beta\)-sheet population than Betanova \((19{-}26\,\%\), aqueous solution). Simultaneous mutations at positions 5, 12 and 17 resulted in peptides that highly populate a \(\beta\)-sheet conformation, both in water \((33{-}45\,\%)\) and in aqueous methanol \((57{-}70\,\%)\). The stability ranking suggested by all indicators of \(\beta\)-sheet population is: Control \(\approx\mathrm{D}5<\mathrm{R}5\approx\mathrm{R}17<\mathrm{D}17\approx\mathrm{Betanova}\approx\mathrm{L}5\approx\mathrm{L}17<\mathrm{Y}5\approx\mathrm{Y}17<\mathrm{L}12\approx\mathrm{F}12<\mathrm{LLM}<\mathrm{YLM}\approx\mathrm{FLM}\).
It has to be mentioned that under our experimental conditions, the \(\beta\)-sheet content estimated from \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) conformational shifts for Betanova (Table 5)
is lower than the population reported previously at lower temperature.5 Molecules that are significantly more stable than the wild-type peptide, as the triple mutants, present, at the most, \(45\,\%\) of folded population in water. This suggests that the population of Betanova at lower temperatures was overestimated.5
There is an excellent correlation between the estimation of \(\upbeta\) -sheet content in buffer and in aqueous methanol (Figure 5(a)). We also found a good correlation between the CD analysis in both media (Table 4). This result indicates that the relative propensities to adopt a \(\upbeta\) -sheet conformation in methanol are merely an amplification of these
propensities in water.22 Water/methanol mixtures should thus provide a more suitable medium than pure water to determine accurately the folded population of the less stable mutants. If we exclude peptides that contain a different number of aromatic residues, we find that the CD and NMR data show equivalent results (Figure 5(b)).
