## \(^1\mathbf{H}\) NMR structural characterisation
Both in aqueous solution and in \(40\,\%\) (v/v) methanol- \(d_{4}/\) water, the \(\upbeta\)-sheet stability ranking based on conformational shifts was supported by the number of relevant medium-range and long-range NOEs observed (Table 6). The analysis of those NOEs indicated that the first \(\upbeta\)-hairpin of all peptides, apart from D5 and R5, folds as required by design. The second \(\upbeta\)-turn, however, has a low tendency to form both for Betanova and its mutants with Asn at position 12. Analysis of F12 and L12 clearly showed that the substitution of Asn12 in Betanova's sequence by Phe or Leu increases both the stability of the first hairpin and the second \(\upbeta\)-turn, as shown by the larger number of medium-range and long-range NOEs found in the second \(\upbeta\)-turn region \((i,i+2\) and \(i,i+3\) contacts) and between cross-strand residues in the first hairpin (2-12; 3-12; 4-11; 5-10) compared to Betanova and any of its mutants at positions 5 and 17.
Figure 6(a) shows a representation of the three-stranded \(\upbeta\)-sheet with the common NOEs found for the triple mutants. Expected \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha i}-\mathrm{H}^{\alpha j}\) NOEs from the structural model were identified unambiguously in the NOESY spectrum, apart from the \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) Lys9- \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) Glu18 NOE (Figure 7(a) and (b)). Long-range NOEs Trp3-Met17, Trp3-Thr16, Tyr10-Met17, Thr11-Met17, Thr16-Thr11 confirmed that mutants LLM, FLM and YLM populate a three-stranded \(\upbeta\)-sheet. Absence of NOEs between the
side-chain and \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) protons of Lys9 and Glu18 suggests that Glu18 does not take a defined orientation and that the end of the third strand is fraying.
A three-dimensional model structure of the triple mutants was calculated in order to check the compatibility of the observed NOEs with the target \(\upbeta\)-sheet. Since the analysis of the NMR parameters indicated that the triple mutants adopt the same \(\upbeta\)-sheet structure in pure water and in \(40\,\%\) methanol, long-range NOEs found in the aqueous/ organic mixture were included in the structure calculation. For peptide LLM, among the 50 best structures, the pairwise RMSD for residues 3-18 was \(0.43(\pm0.14)\) Å for the backbone atoms and \(1.39(\pm0.22)\) Å for all heavy atoms. Although the peptide exhibits the expected antiparallel three-stranded \(\upbeta\)-sheet, the C-terminal residue Glu18 is highly disordered due to the lack of NOE constraints. Figure 8 shows the backbone traces of the ten best structures. The first and last two residues (RG-) are disordered, as expected, because they were added just to avoid aggregation. The overlay of the minimised mean of the ten best structured calculated for peptide LLM with the structure determined by Kornemire et al. for the original Betanova (see Figure 9) points out a different conformation for the aromatic residues Trp3 and Tyr10 in both structures. In the original Betanova, Tyr10 packs against the \(\upbeta\)-turn of the first hairpin, leaving room for Val5 to interact with both Trp3 and Tyr10. In contrast, in peptide LLM, Tyr10 faces Leu5, which interacts instead with residues at the first \(\upbeta\)-turn. Trp3 presents long-range contacts with residues 5, 10, 12 and 17 in the wild-type peptide, whilst in the triple mutant Trp3 interacts mainly with Leu12, presenting some contacts with
