## Experimental versus predicted \(\upbeta\) -sheet stability
Although CD, \(\Delta\delta_{13C\alpha}\) and \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{H}\alpha}\) NMR data showed equivalent results (Figure 5(b) and (c)), we preferred to use \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{H}\alpha}\) data for ranking the peptides in terms of their relative stability (Table 5) because of the smaller experimental error in the measurement of \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) conformational shifts. Thr11 is the indicator of \(\upbeta\) -sheet population with the smallest experimental uncertainties (see Table 5). This is because the \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{H}\alpha}\) value of Thr11 was the largest conformational shift measured. Therefore, even though similar stability estimates were obtained using others NMR probes (Table 5), the \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) conformational shifts of Thr11 yield the most accurate energy values and we used them to compare to the predicted energies. Regarding the energy values obtained from this proton, in aqueous solution
single-point mutants L5 \((-0.23(\pm0.12)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol}\) \((1\;\mathrm{cal}=4.184\;\mathrm{J})\), Y5 \((-0.40(\pm0.11)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\), and Y17 \((-0.40(\pm0.12)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\) are stabilised respect to the original peptide and D5 is clearly destabilised. L17 \((0.00(\pm0.14)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\), D17 \((0.00(\pm0.14)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\), R5 \((+0.09(\pm0.15)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\) and R17 \((+0.20(\pm0.17)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\) do not show any significant stability change compared to Betanova in this medium. In methanol/water mixtures, however, R5 \((+0.25(\pm0.07)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\) and R17 \((+0.14(\pm0.06)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\) are clearly less stable than the wild-type peptide, and L17 \((-0.08(\pm0.06)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\) and D17 \((-0.08(\pm0.06)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol})\) show stability similar to that of Betanova. Those mutants that replace Asn12 by Phe or Leu in the second \(\upbeta\)-strand present an additional stabilisation of 0.60 \((\pm0.11)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol}\), and simultaneous mutations at the three strands stabilise the original peptide between 1.00 \((\pm0.10)\) (LLM) and 1.20 \((\pm0.10)\;\mathrm{kcal/mol}\) (FLM).
Comparison of the NMR-based stability scale with the predicted energies by PERLA shows that the computational method was successful in predicting changes in folding propensity for the single-residue mutants, as well as for the triple mutants (Figure 5(d)). Only the energy prediction for mutation Thr17Arg is in disagreement with the experimental measurements.
## Discussion
Successes in the design of short peptide sequences that fold into \(\upbeta\)-hairpins\(^{29}\) and three-stranded \(\upbeta\)-sheets\(^{30}\) showed that the basic principles behind the formation of these structural elements are being understood and mastered. Nevertheless, due to the complexity of the interactions present in \(\upbeta\)-sheets, any empirical approach similar to those used for \(\upalpha\)-helices\(^{31}\) has important limitations to be applied to the design of \(\upbeta\)-sheets and \(\upbeta\)-hairpins. Hence, properly calibrated algorithms using atomic models to explore the conformation and sequence space shall provide powerful tools to design \(\upbeta\)-sheets or modify their stability. We have tested our computer-based protein design
method, PERLA, re-designing Betanova, a peptide created to fold as a three-stranded \(\upbeta\)-sheet.
Betanova is a 20 residue peptide that could be designed entirely using modern computer algorithms. Our goal, however, was essentially to create a set of peptides with different folding stabilities so that predicted and experimental values could be compared. Thus, we used the sequence of Betanova as template and designed either single-point mutations or triple mutants. For the latter, the residues mutated are located on adjacent \(\upbeta\)-strands on one face of the \(\upbeta\)-sheet. As they interact, re-designing these residues should enable a substantial stabilisation of the peptide structure.
## Estimation of the peptide \(\upbeta\)-sheet content
The quality of the designed sequences was first determined from the peptide apparent structures (the most abundant conformations that contribute most to NOE patterns). At 283 K, the first \(\upbeta\)-hairpin of all peptides, apart from D5 and R5, folds as required by design. The second \(\upbeta\)-turn, however, has a low tendency to form, except for peptides having either Phe or Leu (instead of Asn) at position 12. For the triple mutants, the second \(\upbeta\)-hairpin folds properly, although there is some fraying at the C terminus (residue Glu18).
Finer analyses of the quality of the design were made using estimations of \(\upbeta\)-sheet populations from \(\mathrm{H}^{\alpha}\) conformational shifts. The accurate determination of such populations remains difficult.\(^{32}\) First, there is no method to consider the sequence-dependent distribution of the many conformations that peptides adopt. Consequently, we used the approximation of a two-state folding behaviour, which is the simplest model that is consistent with the experimental data. Nevertheless, if the system presented a multi-state folding behaviour, then the actual energy changes should be larger than the estimates obtained from using a two-state model. Therefore, the energy differences presented here are likely underestimated. Second, no peptide is a perfect model of either of these two states. Yet, for our peptide series, the prospect of large errors is
CD and NMR, both in buffer solutions and in aqueous methanol.
## Origin of the relative stability of Betanova's mutants
The conformational analysis of the designed mutants has shown that mutation of one of the residues of the hydrophobic face of Betanova (Val5) for charged residues (mutants D5 and R5) leads to peptides that are significantly less stable than Betanova. In the light of the energetic terms evaluated by PERLA, mutation Val5Asp is destabilising because the loss of solvation energy of this charged residue upon folding is not compensated with additional electrostatic and/or hydrophobic contacts with respect to the wild-type peptide. Long
aliphatics, as Leu, and aromatic residues, as Tyr, in this position result, however, in more stable \(\upbeta\) - sheet peptides than the original sequence. PERLA's energy function indicates that for Y5 and L5, the
stabilising effect might be due to the increase of van der Waals contacts compared to Betanova. PERLA-modelled structures show that, indeed, extra aliphatic-aromatic interactions with Tyr10, in the case of L5, and aromatic-aromatic interactions, in the case of Y5, are at the origin of the stabilisation.
The second \(\upbeta\) -hairpin of Betanova and single-residue mutants at positions 5 and 17 are poorly structured at \(283~\mathrm{K}\) . Yet mutations of Thr17 have some effect on the overall structural content of the peptide. Substitution of Thr17 by Tyr produces an additional stabilisation of the \(\upbeta\) -sheet conformation due to additional stabilising contacts with Tyr10 and Trp3 (both the structure modelled by PERLA and long-range NOEs support this conclusion). Leu and Asp mutations at position 17 do not change the population significantly and Arg17 destabilises the peptide. These effects are opposite to the predictions made by PERLA. This suggests a situation in which alternative conformations, not considered by the protein design algorithm during the modelling process, could compete with the formation of the desired \(\upbeta\) -hairpin.
One possibility is that the second \(\upbeta\) -turn folds while the following residues remain disordered and do not interact efficiently with the central \(\upbeta\) - strand. In such a case, a major contribution to the stability could originate from medium-range electrostatic interactions, instead of the dominant short-range van der Waals interactions designed in the cluster of Trp3, Tyr10 and residue 17. For peptide D17, a favourable interaction between Lys15 and Asp17 might stabilise the second \(\upbeta\) -turn and
thereby reduce conformational heterogeneity at the C-terminal part of the first \(\upbeta\)-hairpin. The interaction between Lys15 and Arg17 in peptide R17 would have the opposite effect.
Another possibility is that both Asn12 and Asn13 participate in the stabilisation of the second turn, thereby generating a collection of conformations (Asn is a good turn-former). It is known that the sequence Thr11-Asn12-Asn13-Gly14 can form a type I \(\upbeta\)-turn,16 shifting the register of the third strand by one residue, thereby generating a collection of conformations. Examples of \(\upbeta\)-hairpin peptides with alternating turn conformations and strand registers are known.33,34 In agreement with the mentioned hypothesis, mutation Asn12Leu, which should prevent this putative non-native type I \(\upbeta\)-turn, increases the content of correctly folded conformers in that region. Mutations at the second strand (Asn12Leu and Asn12Phe) also lead to the largest stabilising effect observed among the single-point mutations. Likely, it reflects the cooperative stabilisation of both the second \(\upbeta\)-turn and the first \(\upbeta\)-hairpin, as attested by the significant increase of long-range contacts found for both of them compared to the original Betanova.
The triple-point mutants optimise both the folding of the second turn by substituting Asn12 by Leu, and cross-strand interactions on the hydrophobic face of Betanova by aromatic and aliphatic residues at position 5 and an aliphatic residue at position 17, forming a highly hydrophobic core that is probably at the origin of their stability.
The stabilising mutations predicted by PERLA do agree well with the general \(\upbeta\)-sheet propensities. Proposed amino acids as Tyr and Phe are among the most abundant in antiparallel \(\upbeta\)-sheets.35 PERLA did predict stabilising pairs that are not found so frequently:36 at non-hydrogen bonded positions of the \(\upbeta\)-sheet, Trp3-Leu12 (L12 and triple-mutants), Phe5-Tyr10 (FLM), Tyr5-Tyr10 (Y5 and YLM), Leu5-Tyr10 (L5 and LLM), and at hydrogen bonded positions Tyr10-Tyr17 (Y17), Leu12-Lys15 (L12 and triple mutants), and Tyr10-Met17 (triple mutants). This result shows that the program might be used to search for possible stabilising pair combinations within \(\upbeta\)-sheets that cannot be found in natural proteins.
## Application of computer-based protein design to peptide engineering
PERLA could correctly predict the effect of a mutation in the first \(\upbeta\)-hairpin of Betanova with reasonable accuracy, suggesting that the computer program is good for modelling \(\upbeta\)-hairpins. We showed elsewhere that modelling \(\upalpha\)-helices is equally valid.13 However, calculations carried out for mutations in the third \(\upbeta\)-strand, using the modelled three-stranded \(\upbeta\)-sheet structure as template, were not always related to the experimental observations, since one of the mutations (Thr17Arg) was not well predicted (Figure 5(d)). Several reasons could explain this result. Since single mutations in
the first and second strand and simultaneous mutations in the three \(\upbeta\)-strands of the \(\upbeta\)-sheet were predicted successfully, it is unlikely that the force-field and/or the template used for our calculations were at the origin of the failure to predict the stability of this mutant. This might be explained on the basis of the partial unfolding of the second \(\upbeta\)-hairpin of the original Betanova, which could exhibit non-native conformations, as mentioned above.
Failure to predict correctly the effect of all single mutations at position 17 clearly illustrates that a strict inverse folding approximation,37 while central to protein-design computer programs, is not fully satisfying for peptides, because it does not take into account the possible existence of non-native conformations. The optimisation of the interaction of several residues by modelling these positions simultaneously should minimise this problem. In fact, simultaneous mutation of three interacting residues led to three-stranded \(\upbeta\)-sheet peptides that are about 1 kcal/mol more stable than Betanova and that highly populate the intended \(\upbeta\)-sheet conformation. This stabilisation shows that automatic design algorithms can be very helpful for the design of structured peptides, emerging thus as important tools for the design of scaffolds useful for drug discovery.
## Materials and Methods
## Protein design algorithm (PERLA)
PERLA builds selected amino acids in a polypeptide structural template. For this, the program uses a custom-made side-chain rotamer library constructed from the analysis of the protein database, wherein all-atom configurations are given. The occupancy distribution of every side-chain rotamer (for each sequence considered) is determined using the mean-field approximation due to Koehl & Delarue38 using a temperature-simulated annealing. The molecular mechanics energy force-field of ECEPP/2 from Scheraga and co-workers38,39 is used to calculate the interaction energies. The van der Waals, hydrogen bonding and electrostatic (with a distance-dependent dielectric constant of 8.0) interaction energies, are optimised, taking into account the side-chain flexibility by sampling suprotamer configurations (obtained through rotations of \(5^{\circ}\), \(10^{\circ}\) and/or \(15^{\circ}\) around the \(\chi_{1}\) and \(\chi_{2}\) side-chain dihedral angles). The measure of solvation energy changes during the side-chain conformational sampling was adapted from the work of Street & Mayo.40 After sampling, a single conformation defined by the set of the most likely side-chain rotamers is used to re-evaluate the solvation energy from solvent-accessible surface areas (ASA), using the solvation parameters described by Eisenberg & McLachlan.41 ASA calculations are realised with the NSC routine from Eisenhaber.42 Entropy is considered separately for the main chain and side-chains. For the polypeptide backbone, tabulated entropy costs representing losses of conformational freedom are used, as described by Muñoz & Serrano.43 For side-chains, conformational entropy is determined from the probability distribution of all possible rotamers, which is produced from the mean-field approximation sampling method. In this work, the molecular mechanics
force-field energies and entropy changes have been scaled by a factor of 0.5 and the relative dielectric constant used is 16.0.
## Peptide synthesis and purification
Peptides were synthesised using the standard Fmoc solid-phase chemistry. Peptide homogeneity and composition were analysed by analytical HPLC and mass spectrometry.
## Sedimentation equilibrium
Sedimentation equilibrium experiments were performed to obtain the average molecular mass of dilute \((100~\upmu\mathrm{M})\) and concentrated samples \((0.7{-}1\ \mathrm{mM})\) of peptide, both in \(5\;\mathrm{mM}\) sodium acetate buffer \((\mathrm{pH}\,5.0)\) and in buffered \(40\,\%\) \((\mathrm{v/v})\) methanol. Peptide samples were centrifuged at \(283\;\mathrm{K},\) using a Beckman Optima XL-A ultracentrifuge (Beckman Instruments, Inc.) equipped with an An-60 Ti rotor at \(60{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) . Radial scans were taken at different wavelengths every two hours until equilibrium conditions were reached. The data were analysed using the non-linear least-squares approach of the XL-A Ultrascan-Origin (2.98) software package. Only when the solute was treated as a single ideal component did the data yield the expected molecular mass. The quality of the fit was evaluated according to the distribution and magnitude of the residuals corresponding to the difference between the theoretical curve and the experimental data. The specific volume of peptide LLM was \(0.7097\;\mathrm{ml}\;\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) \(0.7005\;\mathrm{ml}\;\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for peptide YLM, for FLM \(0.7043\;\mathrm{ml}\;\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and for the reference peptide DFLM \(0.7195\;\mathrm{ml}\;\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . The density of the solvent was \(1.0004\;\mathrm{g}\) \(\mathrm{ml}^{-1}\) for the pure buffer and \(0.9163\mathrm{~g~ml^{-1}}\) for the buffered \(40\,\%\) \((\mathrm{v/v})\) methanol.
## Far-UV circular dichroism
Stock peptide solutions for CD experiments were prepared in a \(5\;\mathrm{mM}\) sodium acetate \((\mathrm{pH}\ 5.0)\) buffer. The concentration of the peptide samples was determined by measurements of ultraviolet absorbance. Aliquots from the stock solution were taken to obtain samples of concentrations \(100\;\upmu\mathrm{M},\) \(50~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) and \(10~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) . CD spectra were acquired on a Jasco-710 instrument calibrated with (1S)- \((+){-}10\) -camphorsulphonic acid. Usually, 20 scans were acquired in the range \(190{-}250\ \mathrm{nm}\) at a temperature of \(283~\mathrm{K}\) by taking points every \(0.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) , with \(100\ \mathrm{nm\min^{-1}}\) scan-rate, an integration time of one second and a \(1\;\mathrm{nm}\) band-width. Cells with path-lengths of \(0.1\;\mathrm{cm}\) and \(0.5~\mathrm{cm}\) were used for the analysis of peptide concentrations of \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) and \(10~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) , respectively. Methanol titration experiments were carried out for solutions of initial concentration \(50~\upmu\mathrm{M},\) within a \(0.2~\mathrm{cm}\) path-length cell, using \(5~\mathrm{mM}\) sodium acetate \((\mathrm{pH}\ 5.0)\) as a buffer, and absolute methanol as titrator. Methanol was added \((4\times100\;\upmu\mathrm{l})\) to obtain buffered solutions of \(25\,\%\) , \(40\,\%\) , \(50\,\%\) and \(57\,\%\) \((\mathrm{v/v})\) methanol.
## Nuclear magnetic resonance
All NMR experiments were performed on a Brucker DRX500 spectrometer on peptide samples of \({\approx}1{-}2\ \mathrm{mM}\) concentration in pure \(\mathrm{^2H_{2}O}\) or \(90\,\%\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/10\,\%\) \(\mathrm{^2H_{2}O}\) solutions at \(\mathrm{pH}\ 5\) and \(50\,\%\) \(\mathrm{C^{2}H_{3}O^{2}H/50\,\%}\) \(\mathrm{^2H_{2}O}\) or \(50\,\%\) \(\mathrm{C^{2}H_{3}O^{2}H/50\,\%}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . All chemical shifts were internally referenced to the sodium salt of trimethylsilylpropionate
(TSP). Phase-sensitive total correlated spectroscopy (TOCSY) (mixing time \(50~\mathrm{ms}\) ) and nuclear Overhauser enhancement spectroscopy (NOESY)/rotating frame Overhauser effect spectroscopy (ROESY) (mixing time 150, 200 and \(250~\mathrm{ms}\) ) experiments were performed at \(283\;\mathrm{K},\) collecting 2000 points in \(\mathcal{f}2\) and 512 points in f1. Solvent suppression was achieved by selective presaturation during the relaxation delay (1.2 s) or field-gradient pulses. The proton resonances were assigned by the sequential assignment procedure.44
## Structure calculation
NOE intensities were evaluated qualitatively and used to obtain upper limit distance constraints. NOEs were classified as strong (strong \(\leqslant3.0\) ), between strong and medium \((3.0<\) strong-medium \(\leqslant3.5\) ), medium \((3.5<\) medium \(\leqslant4.0\) ), between medium and weak \((4.0<\) medium-weak \(\leqslant4.5\) ), weak \((4.5<\) weak \(\leqslant5.0\) ) and very weak \((5.0<\) very weak \(\leqslant6.0\) ). For all triple mutants, \(\phi\) angles were constrained to the range \(-180^{\circ}\) to \(-20^{\circ}\) , except for Asn7, Gly8, Asn13 and Gly14. With a total of 116 (LLM), 110 (YLM) and 130 (FLM) upperlimit distance restraints derived from NOEs, a family of 50 structures without NOE violations larger than \(0.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\) was generated using DYANA (dynamics algorithm for NMR applications).45 For peptide LLM, the pairwise RMSD for residues 3-18 was \(0.43(\pm0.14)\) Å for the backbone atoms and \(1.39(\pm0.22)\) Å for all heavy atoms; for peptide YLM, the pairwise RMSD for residues 3-18 was \(0.31(\pm0.14)\) Å for the backbone atoms and \(1.68(\pm0.27)\) Å for all heavy atoms, and for peptide FLM, the pairwise RMSD for residues 3-18 was \(0.86(\pm0.40)\) Å for the backbone atoms and \(1.97(\pm0.63)\) Å for all heavy atoms. Overlay and mean of the ten best structures calculated using DYANA45 was obtained using MOLMOL.46 Energy minimisation of the mean structure was done with the GROMOS96 implementation of Swiss-PdbViewer. Overlay and RMSD between LLM and Betanova's NMR structures, and between the experimental and predicted structures for LLM were done with MOLMOL.46 Figures showing 3D structures were prepared with RASMOL (Figure 1), MOLMOL46 (Figure 8) and SwissPdbViewer (Figures 9 and 10).
Calculation of \(\upbeta\) -sheet population and \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) from \(\mathsf{H}^{\mathsf{a}}\) conformational chemical shifts
The equilibrium constant \((K_{\mathrm{eq}})\) was estimated via equation (1):
\[
K_{\mathrm{eq}}=(\delta_{\mathrm{obs}}-\delta_{\mathrm{U}})/(\delta_{\mathrm{F}}-\delta_{\mathrm{obs}})
\]
where \(\delta_{\mathrm{obs}}\) is the measured chemical shift of the considered proton within the peptide; \(\delta_{\mathrm{U}}\) is the chemical shift of that proton in the fully unfolded state (reference \(\upbeta\) -strand peptides) and \(\delta_{\mathrm{F}}\) is the chemical shift of that proton in the fully folded state \((^{1}\mathrm{FLM})\) . \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) was calculated via equation (2):
\[
\Delta G^{\circ}=-RT\ln K_{\mathrm{eq}}
\]
where \(R\) is the gas constant and \(T\) is the temperature \((283\;\mathrm{K})\) . \(\Delta\Delta G^{\circ}=\Delta G^{\circ}\) (mutant) \(-\Delta G^{\circ}\) (Betanova). The population of the \(\upbeta\) -sheet state for each peptide was calculated at each indicator residue from \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{Hz}}\) data via equation (3):
\[
[(\delta_{\mathrm{obs}}-\delta_{\mathrm{U}})/(\delta_{\mathrm{F}}-\delta_{\mathrm{U}})]\times100
\]
Experimental uncertainties reflect error propagation from the \(\pm0.01\) ppm uncertainty in the \(\delta_{\mathrm{Hz}}\) measurement.
## Additional information
Far-UV CD methanol tritrations, \(\mathrm{H^{+}}\) and HN chemical shift profile (buffered solution and \(40\,\%\) (v/v) methanol), and \(K_{\mathrm{eq}}\) and \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) values obtained from \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{Hz}}(\mathrm{Lys9})\), \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{Hz}}(\mathrm{Tyr10})\), \(\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{Hz}}(\mathrm{Thr11})\) and \(\mathrm{RMS}\Delta\delta_{\mathrm{Hz}}\) for all mutants and reference peptides, a Table of medium-range and long-range NOEs for Betanova, L12 and triple-point mutants and coordinates of the structure calculated for the triple-mutants are available from the authors upon request.
